\caption{Fan-vote flip elasticity: final placement of each focal contestant under rules
R and P as the fan-vote multiplier scales from $\times 1$ (base) through $\times 16$.}
\label{fig:m2flip}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Fan-vote flip elasticity}

Figure~\ref{fig:m2flip} reports the flip experiment: multiplying the focal celebrity's
$\hat\phi$ by a factor $c \in \{2, 4, 8, 16\}$ from week 1 onward and replaying both
rules. The percentage rule is strictly more responsive to fan surges in four of the six
controversy cases. Two results stand out:
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=1pt]
\item \textbf{Bristol Palin (S11):} under the rank rule she never wins the replay for any
multiplier, but under the percentage rule she is runner-up at $\times 2$ and champion from
$\times 4$ onward.
\item \textbf{Bobby Bones (S27):} neither rule reproduces his observed win at any
multiplier, but the rank rule is more resistant to the fan surge (4th at $\times 2$
under R vs.\ runner-up under P) --- the rank rule decouples outcomes from fan votes more
effectively, which is precisely why the producers' choice of combination rule matters.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Which rule is more judge-consistent, and which favors fans?}

Table~\ref{tab:rule-metrics} reports the three recommendation metrics averaged over all
34 seasons: (i) \emph{judge consistency}, the Kendall $\tau$ between final placement and
mean judge share (a rule approaching $-1$ best matches the judges' aggregate ranking);
(ii) \emph{fan-voice intensity}, the Gini coefficient of the fan-share distribution over
contestant-weeks; and (iii) the \emph{shock rate}, the fraction of weeks in which a
top-3-by-$\hat\phi$ couple is eliminated.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Recommendation metrics per combination rule, averaged over 34 seasons.}
\label{tab:rule-metrics}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Rule & Judge-consistency $\tau$ & Fan-voice Gini & Top-3-$\hat\phi$ shock rate \\
\midrule
R (rank)       & $-0.756$ & 0.546 & 3.8\% \\
P (percentage) & $-0.618$ & 0.559 & 0.3\% \\
Z (z-scored)   & $-0.799$ & 0.530 & 5.0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:m2tauvsqini} plots the same trade-off per season. The z-scored rule Z is
the most judge-consistent ($\tau = -0.799$), the rank rule intermediate
($-0.756$), and the percentage rule the least judge-consistent ($-0.618$); the percentage
rule is therefore the one that \emph{decouples outcomes most from judge quality} --- which
is exactly why fan surges can flip a percentage-rule replay, and equally why the shock
collapse (0.3\% vs.\ 3.8\%/5.0\%) makes P the most protective of fan favorites.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/F6_tau_vs_gini.png}}
\caption{Judge-consistency $\tau$ versus fan-voice Gini per rule, all 34 seasons
(each point a season). The R--Z cluster sits high-tau; the P cluster drifts toward
fan-voice dominance.}
\label{fig:m2tauvsqini}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/F9_validation_by_season.png}}
\caption{Era-correct replay validation: per-season cut-match rate. S1--2 (rank, 100\%)
and S3--27 (percentage, 99.2\%) validate the engine; S28+ (49.0\%) reflects the
unmodeled judges' dance-off.}
\label{fig:m2validation}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/F12_hdi_sensitivity.png}}
\caption{HDI-bound sensitivity: controversy placements at the 90\% band edges of the
focal celebrity's $\hat\phi$. Seven of eight verdicts are fully robust; the single
adverse edge is Bobby Bones under P (3rd at the base and at the upper edge, 4th at the
lower edge).}
\label{fig:m2hdi}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/F11_danceoff_flips.png}}
\caption{Dance-off overlay (S28+): per-season counts of elimination weeks in which adding
the judges' choice stage changes the cut, and the number of couples whose placement
shifts. The stage changes the cut in 2--5 of 6--9 elimination weeks per season and shifts
5--11 placements.}
\label{fig:m2danceoff}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Headline: rank vs.\ percentage across all 34 seasons}

Pooled over all 265 elimination weeks in the 34 seasons, the two rules produce
\emph{different} elimination sets in 129 weeks (48.7\% weekly disagreement). Because
Method Z (z-scored) recovers the observed record in S3--27 nearly as well as the era-correct
rule but was never actually adopted, the practical question for producers is not
``which rule is right'' but ``which disagreements are desirable'' --- and
Table~\ref{tab:rule-metrics} says the percentage rule's extra fan voice comes at the cost
of shock-elimination protection, while the z-scored rule buys judge consistency at 5.0\%
shock rate. Figure~\ref{fig:m2gamma} summarizes the season-by-season disagreement.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/F4_gamma_by_season.png}}
\caption{Weekly rule-disagreement rate $\gamma_s$ per season (mean 0.468, median 0.444).
Three seasons (S1, S25, S26) show zero disagreement --- the two rules are
interchangeable there --- while season 9 peaks at 0.889.}
\label{fig:m2gamma}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.88\textwidth]{{../02_programming/model_2/figures/F8_bandwidth_by_week.png}}
\caption{Median relative 90\%-band width of $\hat\phi$ by week-in-season (certainty by
week): bands widen as the cast shrinks and the season deepens.}
\label{fig:m2bandwidth}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Validation}

The S28+ decomposition, the era-correct replay (Table~\ref{tab:replay-valid}), the
controversy directions matching historical documentation (Table~\ref{tab:controversies}),
and three zero-$\gamma_s$ crossover seasons together confirm the engine produces genuine
rule effects rather than model-induced artifacts. Reproduction parameters trade off on a
composite ridge; the reported replay numbers use the higher-likelihood member and the
runner-up member produces empirically similar behavior. All replays hold the elimination
calendar fixed at observed quotas (counterfactuals change \emph{who} is cut, never
\emph{when} or how many). Party appeal is bounded in $[-2.75, +2.5] \subset [-3, 3]$,
so the softmax normalization is never saturated.

